    "created_at": "2025-04-08T15:37:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-04-08T15:37:43Z",
    "body": "Here are the implementations of `window.close` in [Blink DOMWindow::Close](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/dom_window.cc;l=602-658;drc=3950ca093c2f4e1d1f663cfc047413c375e47111), [WebKit DOMWindow::close](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/59247a7628dd45966bd71f2a00523ed891650aa7/Source/WebCore/page/DOMWindow.cpp#L103-L129), [Gecko nsGlobalWindowOuter::CloseOuter](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/9c6e9ca72d4a59267d985fb35ba259fb937c8503/dom/base/nsGlobalWindowOuter.cpp#5895-5966).\n\n---\n\nRegarding the first half of #script-closable\n>  auxiliary browsing context that was created by a script (as opposed to by an action of the user)\n\nGecko tracks this as `TopLevelCreatedByWebContent`; Blink and WebKit through `OpenedByDOM`. I think in all browsers is it set when a page is created through a non-auxiliary link click or through `window.open`. Maybe the spec could introduce a similar flag on browsing contexts, or at least include link-initiated navigations in the #script-closable definition.\n\n---\n\nPR https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/6315 changed the second part of #script-closable from\n> or if it is a top-level browsing context whose session history contains only one Document\n\nto\n> or if it is a top-level traversable whose session history entries's size is 1.\n\nhopefully this clears up previous concerns with the session history check? Blink and WebKit implement the current behavior, Gecko still follows the old definition. I'm unsure if this is covered by wpt, maybe a test should be added to `/html/browsers/the-window-object/open-close`?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
